Haiku kernel: fix "VMArea::Unwire(...): no such range" panic when
lock_memory_etc() fails part-way through an area.

This panic killed the content_shell link twice on 2026-09-01/02, both times in
thread "ld" while writing the ~190 MB output file, and it is what made the
final link unreachable on this machine:

    PANIC: VMArea::Unwire(0x5e1f8000, 0x2000, 0): no such range
    Thread 2070 "ld" running on CPU 0
      ...
      VMArea<...>::Unwire(uint32: 0x5e1f8000, uint32: 0x2000 (8192), false)
      unlock_memory_etc + 0x3e1
      lock_memory_etc + 0x692
      IOBuffer<...>::LockMemory(int32: 2070, true)
      IOSchedulerSimple<...>::ScheduleRequest(...)
      ...
      bfs_write / file_write / common_user_io(..., 0x28000 (163840), true)

Mechanism, from lock_memory_etc() in src/system/kernel/vm/vm.cpp:

1. For each area the request spans, it allocates a wired range covering that
   area's whole portion:

       VMAreaWiredRange(areaStart, areaEnd - areaStart, writable, true)

   and registers it with area->Wire(range) *before* touching any page.

2. It then walks the pages one at a time, either bumping an already-mapped
   page's wired count or calling vm_soft_fault() to map it. Under memory
   pressure vm_soft_fault() can fail, and the loop breaks at nextAddress,
   somewhere strictly inside the area's portion.

3. The cleanup only fully unwires the range when nextAddress == areaStart.
   Otherwise it leaves the range registered at its original size and falls
   through to:

       unlock_memory_etc(team, (void*)lockBaseAddress,
           nextAddress - lockBaseAddress, flags);

4. unlock_memory_etc() reverts exactly [lockBaseAddress, nextAddress) and so
   calls area->Unwire(areaStart, nextAddress - areaStart, writable). The
   registered range still has size (areaEnd - areaStart). VMArea::Unwire()
   matches on (base, size, writable) exactly, finds nothing, and panics.

The panic arguments match this exactly. The write was 0x28000 (163840 bytes,
40 pages), so lock_memory_etc() wired a 40-page range; it failed after 2 pages,
and unlock_memory_etc() then asked to unwire 0x2000 (8192 bytes, 2 pages) --
"no such range".

Fix: in that error path, shrink the registered range to the part that was
actually wired before releasing the cache lock. The pages in
[areaStart, nextAddress) are precisely the ones whose wired count was
incremented, so the shrunk range describes the true state; unlock_memory_etc()
then finds it and unwinds correctly.

Shrinking is safe for the range's other consumers: apart from the exact-match
lookup in VMArea::Unwire(base, size, writable), size is only read through
VMAreaWiredRange::IntersectsWith() (VMArea::IsWired and the AddWaiterIfWired
pair), where a range that no longer claims pages it never wired is strictly
more accurate.

Not reported upstream (this project has no upstream access); recorded here.

--- a/src/system/kernel/vm/vm.cpp
+++ b/src/system/kernel/vm/vm.cpp
@@ -5194,8 +5194,19 @@
 				cacheChainLocker.Unlock();
 				range->~VMAreaWiredRange();
 				free_etc(range, mallocFlags);
-			} else
+			} else {
+				// We wired the range covering the whole of this area's portion
+				// of the request, but only got as far as nextAddress before
+				// failing. The unlock_memory_etc() call below reverts exactly
+				// [lockBaseAddress, nextAddress), so it looks for a range of
+				// size (nextAddress - areaStart); leaving the recorded size at
+				// (areaEnd - areaStart) makes that lookup find nothing and
+				// panic with "no such range". Shrink it to what is actually
+				// wired -- the pages in [areaStart, nextAddress) are the only
+				// ones whose wired count we incremented.
+				range->size = nextAddress - areaStart;
 				cacheChainLocker.Unlock();
+			}
 
 			break;
 		}
